  "worked_solution": "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
